!********************************************************************** 
!   System:                     C3H7NO
!   Functional form:            permutationally invariant polynomials
!   Common name:                N-methyl acetamide
!   Number of derivatives:      1
!   Number of bodies:           12
!   Number of electronic surfaces: 1
!   Interface: Section-2
!**********************************************************************
!
!   References: A. Nandi, C. Qu, and J. M. Bowman "Full and fragmented
!   permutationally invariant polynomial potential energy surfaces for
!   trans and cis N-methyl acetamide and isomerization saddle points"
!   J. Chem. Phys. 2019, 151, 084306.
!
!   Notes: See the notes below
!
!  H   xyz(1, 1) xyz(2, 1) xyz(3, 1)
!  H   xyz(1, 2) xyz(2, 2) xyz(3, 2)
!  H   xyz(1, 3) xyz(2, 3) xyz(3, 3)
!  C   xyz(1, 4) xyz(2, 4) xyz(3, 4)
!  N   xyz(1, 5) xyz(2, 5) xyz(3, 5)
!  H   xyz(1, 6) xyz(2, 6) xyz(3, 6)
!  O   xyz(1, 7) xyz(2, 7) xyz(3, 7)
!  C   xyz(1, 8) xyz(2, 8) xyz(3, 8)
!  C   xyz(1, 9) xyz(2, 9) xyz(3, 9)
!  H   xyz(1,12) xyz(2,12) xyz(3,12)
!  H   xyz(1,11) xyz(2,11) xyz(3,11)
!  H   xyz(1,12) xyz(2,12) xyz(3,12)
!
!
!   Input: xyz(3,12)                     in units of Angstrom
!   Output: V                            in units of hartree
!   Output: g(36)                        in units hartree/bohr
!**********************************************************************


This is a PES for N-methyl acetamide (trans and cis isomer and two TS), using permutationally invariant polynomial fitting basis.

The polynomials are defined in basis.f90
The derivatives of the polynomials with respect to Cartesian coordinates are computed in gradient.f90
The function/subroutine to calculate the potential and gradients are defined in pes_shell.f90

Compile with the Makefile provided (if you use the Intel ifort compiler, otherwise modify the Makefile)
and run the executable as
./test.x geom.xyz

It will calculate the potential energy and the gradient of the configuration in geom.xyz.
The first line of geom.xyz is the number of atoms; the second line is the ab initio potential energy;
the following lines consist of the atomic symbol, followed by Cartesian coordinates, followed by the
gradients.
Note that the Cartesian coordinates are in unit Angstrom and the order of the atoms is
H H H C N H O C C H H H, see Figure 2 of the paper for the labels and numbers.

